@module flutter-m17 | Performance
@summary Understand the rendering pipeline and optimise rebuilds, images, lists, memory and startup.

@lesson flutter-m17-l01 | Rendering pipeline | 8
## Learning objectives
- Know where frame time goes

## Rendering pipeline
Each frame: build → layout → paint on the UI thread, then rasterisation on the raster thread. You have ~16 ms (60 Hz) or ~8 ms (120 Hz) per frame.

```dart
// UI thread: build, layout, paint. Raster thread: GPU work.
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Both threads must finish within the frame budget.

@lesson flutter-m17-l02 | Widget rebuild optimization | 8
## Learning objectives
- Rebuild less

## Widget rebuild optimization
Use const widgets, split large widgets, push state down so only small subtrees rebuild, and use selectors (`context.select`, `ref.watch(provider.select(...))`).

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget header() => const Padding(padding: EdgeInsets.all(16), child: Text('Static header'));
```

## Summary
- const and smaller widgets reduce rebuild work.

@lesson flutter-m17-l03 | Image optimization | 8
## Learning objectives
- Load images efficiently

## Image optimization
Resize images to display size (`cacheWidth`), use compressed formats (WebP), cache network images and precache important ones.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget thumb(String url) => Image.network(url, width: 96, cacheWidth: 192, fit: BoxFit.cover);
```

## Summary
- Decode images at the size you display.

@lesson flutter-m17-l04 | List optimization | 8
## Learning objectives
- Scroll long lists smoothly

## List optimization
Use builder constructors, `itemExtent`/`prototypeItem` for fixed heights, keys for stable items and avoid heavy work in itemBuilder.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget list(List<String> items) => ListView.builder(itemCount: items.length, itemExtent: 56, itemBuilder: (_, i) => ListTile(title: Text(items[i])));
```

## Summary
- Builders + fixed extents scale to huge lists.

@lesson flutter-m17-l05 | Memory usage | 8
## Learning objectives
- Keep memory low

## Memory usage
Limit image cache size, release large objects, paginate data and watch the Memory view for growth.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/painting.dart';

void limitCache() => PaintingBinding.instance.imageCache.maximumSizeBytes = 50 << 20;
```

## Summary
- Bound caches and paginate.

@lesson flutter-m17-l06 | Async performance | 8
## Learning objectives
- Keep heavy work off the UI isolate

## Async performance
Move CPU-heavy parsing to `compute`/`Isolate.run` and avoid awaiting in sequence when work can run concurrently.

```dart
import 'dart:convert';
import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart';

Future<Object?> parse(String json) => compute(jsonDecode, json);
```

## Summary
- Use isolates for heavy CPU work.

@lesson flutter-m17-l07 | App startup performance | 8
## Learning objectives
- Start fast

## App startup performance
Do minimal work before the first frame: defer initialisation, load lazily, avoid big synchronous reads in main, and keep the splash short.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(const MaterialApp(home: Text('First frame fast')));
  // initialise analytics etc. after the first frame
}
```

## Summary
- Show UI first, initialise the rest later.

@lesson flutter-m17-l08 | Profiling | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use DevTools to find bottlenecks

## Profiling
Record a timeline in profile mode, find slow frames, inspect the CPU flame chart and enable 'track widget rebuilds'.

```dart
// flutter run --profile → DevTools → Performance → Record
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Measure before optimising.

@lesson flutter-m17-l09 | Release performance | 8
## Learning objectives
- Ship optimised builds

## Release performance
Release builds are AOT-compiled and tree-shaken. Use `--split-debug-info` and `--obfuscate`, and test release builds on low-end devices.

```dart
// flutter build appbundle --release --obfuscate --split-debug-info=build/symbols
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Test performance on real, low-end hardware.
